# What is not source.
#
# The rule this file follows: **ignore what a command can make again, keep
# what it cannot.**  Two kinds of file in this tree look like output and
# are not, and both are called out below rather than left to a pattern.

# ── Python ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/

# ── The suite's own report ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `tools/suite.py` writes this.  Generated, and deliberately not kept:
# `pytest.ini` says a golden test is bit-exact where the buffer was made
# and nowhere else, so a report is a claim about one machine at one
# commit.  Committed, it would be read on a second machine as though it
# meant something there.
test/report.md

# Virtualenvs, whatever they are called.
.venv/
venv/
env/

# ── Native build output ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `audiollvm.build()` shells out to clang and `audiohost.Host` compiles
# `host.c`; both write into a temporary directory, but a run interrupted at
# the wrong moment can leave one of these behind.  `host.c` itself is
# source and is not matched by any of these.

*.so
*.o
*.ll
*.bc
*.dylib
*.dll

# ── Rendered audio ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Sixteen megabytes of it at the time of writing, every byte of which
# `python -m gestate.audio <file> -o <out>.wav` will make again from the
# `.ges` beside it.

*.wav
*.mp3
*.flac
*.ogg

# ── The Rust workspace ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `crust/` and `shell/clap` each ignore their own `target/`; these two
# cover a cargo run from the root.
#
# **`Cargo.lock` is tracked, and the rule that once ignored it expired.**
# The comment here used to read "both crates are zero-dependency by rule,
# so the lock pins nothing a command cannot make again."  That was true of
# a two-member workspace.  It has four members now — `shell/editor` and
# `shell/panel` joined later and brought `baseview`, `softbuffer`,
# `raw-window-handle`, `keyboard-types` and `libc`, which resolve
# transitively to **133 crates**.  Nothing caught the drift because the
# justification kept reading as authoritative after the thing it asserted
# had stopped being true.
#
# The rule itself is alive and still worth its keep: `crust` and
# `shell/clap` take nothing that is not `optional`, which is what lets a
# host load the plugin from an offline build.  What expired was
# generalising it to the whole tree.
#
# Why the lock has to be in the tree now: cargo runs **build scripts and
# proc-macros as arbitrary code at compile time** — `cc`, `ctor`,
# `bytemuck_derive`, `drm-sys` and `jni-sys-macros` are all in that set.
# Unpinned, an ordinary `cargo build` may resolve a fresh patch release of
# any of the 133 and execute it.  A tracked lock makes the build
# reproducible and gives `cargo audit` a fixed set to check.

/target/

# ── Editor and OS litter ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

*~
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# ── The atlas's raster, which the .svg can make again ────────────────────
#
# `doc/atlas/whole.svg` is committed — a generated file kept, like
# `doc/ref/`, because the point of it is to be read without running
# anything.  The `.png` beside it is not, for one reason: **the test
# cannot check it.**  `test_atlas.py` fails when the committed sheet is
# not what the source renders, and that comparison is only honest for
# text — raster bytes differ between rasterisers, and between versions
# of one.  A generated file with no such guarantee is the drift the
# atlas was built to prevent, wearing a different suffix.
#
# `python -m gestate.atlas` makes it in about a second whenever anybody
# wants to look at the sheet in a viewer that cannot open a vector.
doc/atlas/*.png

# ── Three things that are NOT ignored, deliberately ──────────────────────
#
# **`doc/gestate.argb` — the icon's pixels.**  Generated, by `python -m
# gestate.icon`, and kept for a reason the atlas's `.png` above does not
# have: **`shell/editor` compiles it in.**  `include_bytes!` means an
# ignored copy is a fresh clone that does not build, which is a worse
# failure than a stale drawing.  And the objection that keeps the
# atlas's raster out does not apply here — those bytes come from a
# rasteriser nobody in this tree controls, these come from
# `gestate/icon.py`, so `test_icon.py` can and does check them against
# what the source renders today.
#
# **`*.samples` — the golden buffers.**  They look like build output and
# are the opposite: each one is `render()` pinned, and it is the *oracle*
# three engines are checked against.  `test_audio.py` fails outright
# without them, and regenerating one does not restore it — it re-records
# whatever the code does today, which is precisely the question the file
# exists to answer.  Never add a rule for these.
#
# **`*.mid` — not all of them can be made again.**  Most of
# `examples/music/` regenerates from the `.ges` beside it, so a blanket
# rule looks safe.  It is not:
#
#   * `examples/contrib/neon_yossa.mid` has no `.ges` at all;
#   * the other three files in `examples/contrib/` were MIDI pieces and
#     have since been given gestate instruments, so `gestate.midi` no
#     longer renders them — their `.mid` is now the only copy of what they
#     sounded like before.
#
# Four of the twelve are therefore irreplaceable, and a pattern cannot tell
# which four.  So none of them are ignored; a genuinely disposable render
# is better deleted than pattern-matched.
